{#- engine_clean_window: the one block ExecuTorch overrides for a MEASUREMENT
    reason rather than an API one.

    nsx::executorch::run_once_profiled() reloads and re-initialises the program
    on every call and returns `execution_cycles` measured by the runtime around
    the execute step alone.  The base's default window brackets
    self.engine_invoke() with the window clock, which for this engine would
    silently redefine HPX_CLEAN_INFER_* as load+execute -- a semantics change
    the wire protocol has no way to express.  So the clean window accumulates
    result.execution_cycles instead, keeping HPX_CLEAN_INFER_* execute-only and
    byte-for-byte comparable with what this template reported before it became
    a child.

    Two consequences worth stating rather than leaving to be discovered:

      * The base's per-pass profiled loop still DWT-brackets engine_invoke,
        which for this engine measures whole-run_once cost (load + execute),
        not the execute-only cost every other engine reports under the same
        HPX_PROFILED_INFER_* keys.  Nothing downstream would catch that: the
        comparability model treats ComparisonDimension.ENGINE as INFORMATIVE
        (results/dimensions.py -- a difference is reported, never blocking; the
        only identity dimension is MODEL_SHA256), so two engines' runs of the
        same model compare freely, and validation/runner.py falls back to
        device_profiled_infer_avg_us as the run latency when the clean number
        is absent -- silently substituting load+execute for inference time.  So
        ExecuTorch emits no HPX_PROFILED_INFER_* line at all (its
        engine_profiled_summary override only (void)-reads the accumulators,
        #161), exactly as it did before the template unification.  The
        profiled_infer_cycles accumulation in the shared loop still runs.
      * The base gates the CoreSight debug power domain off for the measured
        window on families where the window clock does not live there
        (gate_debug_domain_in_window, AP5).  ExecuTorch's window clock is the
        runtime's own cycle counter, which this template cannot prove is
        independent of that domain, so this override does NOT call
        am_hal_debug_disable(): freezing the counter HPX_CLEAN_INFER_* is
        derived from is exactly the #106/#107 bug class.  Releasing the ARM PMU
        counters is kept -- that is pure win with no clock exposure.  Nothing
        is lost by this: PD_DBG gating exists to keep a POWER window clean, and
        ExecuTorch has no power binary.

    The busy_loop probe is a power-window diagnostic whose window body runs no
    inference at all (a calibrated nop loop, timed by the STIMER bracket), so
    it is engine-independent and delegates straight back to the base via
    super() -- both to keep the one code path and so the calibration the base
    emits above this block can never become an unused variable. -#}
{% block engine_clean_window %}{% if busy_loop_probe %}{{ super() }}{% else %}
{% if has_armv8m_pmu %}
        // Release the ARM PMU counters for the clean window (no PMU traffic
        // during the measurement).  Deliberately NOT paired with
        // am_hal_debug_disable() here -- see this block's template comment.
        nsx_ambiq_pmu_disable();
{% endif %}
        // Execute-only cycles, reported by the ExecuTorch runtime itself.  The
        // host sees the same HPX_CLEAN_INFER_* semantics this engine has always
        // had; run_once_profiled()'s per-call model load is deliberately NOT in
        // this total.
        uint64_t clean_exec_cycles = 0;
        hpx_sync_window_begin();
        for (int iter = 0; iter < clean_iters_n; iter++) {
            const nsx::executorch::RunResult result = run_model();
            require_ok(result);
            clean_exec_cycles += result.execution_cycles;
            clean_count++;
{% if clean_window_trace | default(false) and transport not in ["swo", "uart"] %}
            // Sanity-check trace: prove the device is looping inferences for
            // the whole gated window.  Perturbs power — diagnostic use only.
            // Excluded on SWO/UART: their printf peripherals are disabled
            // above for the same window (nsx_itm/uart_printf_disable()).
            hpx_printf("HPX_CLEAN_ITER=%d\n", iter);
{% endif %}
        }
        hpx_sync_window_end();
{% if has_armv8m_pmu %}
        // Restore the PMU for the instrumented per-layer passes below.
        nsx_ambiq_pmu_enable();
{% endif %}
{% if transport == "uart" %}
        nsx_uart_printf_enable();
        nsx_delay_us(HPX_DEBUG_REENABLE_SETTLE_US);
{% elif transport == "swo" %}
        nsx_itm_printf_enable();
        nsx_delay_us(HPX_DEBUG_REENABLE_SETTLE_US);
{% elif transport == "usb_cdc" %}
        usb_timer_resume();
{% endif %}
        if (clean_count > 0) {
            const uint32_t clean_avg_cycles =
                (uint32_t)((clean_exec_cycles + (clean_count / 2U)) / clean_count);
            hpx_printf("HPX_CLEAN_INFER_COUNT=%lu\n", (unsigned long)clean_count);
            hpx_printf("HPX_CLEAN_INFER_TOTAL_CYCLES=%llu\n",
                       (unsigned long long)clean_exec_cycles);
            hpx_printf("HPX_CLEAN_INFER_AVG_CYCLES=%lu\n",
                       (unsigned long)clean_avg_cycles);
            if (SystemCoreClock > 0U) {
                const uint32_t clean_avg_us = (uint32_t)(
                    ((uint64_t)clean_avg_cycles * 1000000ULL +
                     ((uint64_t)SystemCoreClock / 2ULL)) /
                    (uint64_t)SystemCoreClock);
                hpx_printf("HPX_CLEAN_INFER_AVG_US=%lu\n",
                           (unsigned long)clean_avg_us);
            }
        }{% endif %}{% endblock %}

